  "details": "As is with the new Dynamics, I'm finding myself wanting to do a lot with knowing what the avatar's doing in terms of bone rotation to drive things. How I'm doing this is a absurd system of Contacts set to Proximity with specific geometry to the sender/receiver so I can get a pesudo-angle read of whatever that bone is doing, but only for 90 degrees of motion, so I have to double that up for a single 180 degrees of motion, then 3 times more if I need all three axis, then double THAT if I wanted 360 degrees in all directions, for one bone. Suffice to say, the Contacts count gets absurd fast.\nWhat I'm proposing is a component which all it does is report a -1 to 1 float value, one for each of the three axis, of a given bone/object's orientation offset relative to how far it moved from it's original set position in the editor. \nWhich before anyone suggests using Physbone's “_Angle” for this, I tried. Even if tripling up the number of bones/empties for each axis wouldn't get obnoxious fast, they just straight up ignore rotation constraints no matter what settings I set the Physbone component to, and making a arm or leg floppy when it's not supposed to be floppy isn't exactly useful for most use cases.",
